2	 *
3	 * The andesim runtime routes newlib syscalls through MMIO devices both
4	 * engines model, so the SAME ELF gets working stdio, file io and exit
5	 * codes on fast (QEMU), cycle (vsim) and hybrid:
6	 *
7	 *   stdout        UART2 (0xf0300020) -- printf goes to your terminal
8	 *   file io       HTIF magicmem (0xe0080010), served host-side: fopen/
9	 *                 fread/fwrite hit REAL files in the working directory
10	 *   time          gettimeofday(), same HTIF channel, host clock
11	 *   exit code     SimControl (0xe0080000) -- main's return value becomes
12	 *                 the andesim exit code on fast and cycle
13	 *
14	 * Hybrid adds two rules, which this demo demonstrates:
15	 *   - an open FILE* must be CLOSED before the handoff. Host file
16	 *     descriptors live in the engine process and do not cross to vsim;
17	 *     a closed file lives on the host filesystem, which both engines
18	 *     share.
19	 *   - a hybrid run ENDS at the ROI exit trigger (that is where the
20	 *     cycle figure is drained), so code after the ROI never runs on
21	 *     hybrid. Anything that must happen -- here the file cleanup --
22	 *     belongs inside the ROI; the hybrid pass/fail signal is the
23	 *     printed SYSCALL_OK, not main's return value.
24	 *
25	 * QEMU phase (before the ROI):
26	 *   - gettimeofday() seeds a payload no compiler can precompute
27	 *   - fwrite the payload to syscall_demo.tmp, fclose it
28	 *
29	 * vsim ROI:
30	 *   - printf INSIDE the ROI (its UART cycles are simply part of the
31	 *     figure)
32	 *   - fopen/fread the file back, compare with the in-DRAM copy (the copy
33	 *     crossed the handoff in the shared mmap; the file crossed on the
34	 *     host filesystem), remove it; prints SYSCALL_OK / SYSCALL_BAD
35	 */
36	
